The PIT raises the IRQ 0 edge the guest is owed before it reads the
counter.

pit_get_count() computes the count from QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL at every
access, but the edge at the wrap is raised by irq_timer, which the main
loop runs a wakeup late -- and a guest reading the PIT in a tight loop
holds the BQL for most of that. A read in the gap sees the counter wrapped
and no interrupt behind it, which a real 8254 shows for about one clock.
Anything that keeps time from the BIOS tick count plus the counter reads
that as time going backward: DOS Quake's Sys_FloatTime counts a backward
step as zero *and* takes it as the new reference, so the period is counted
again once the tick lands. Measured with guest-tools' QCLOCK.COM in pure
DOS: one full 55 ms backward step on every tick, 540 of 540, and Quake's
clock at exactly twice real time.

Every port access now first delivers what is overdue (the transitions
whose time has passed and irq_timer has not run yet), in order, so the
PIC sees the edge it would have seen. An IN or OUT ends its TB
(translator_io_start), so the interrupt is taken before the guest's next
instruction -- Quake's own "the tick moved during the read" check then
does exactly what it does on the real part.

-global isa-pit.overdue-irq=off restores upstream's behaviour (the A/B).

diff --git a/hw/timer/i8254.c b/hw/timer/i8254.c
index 1a8b6bf9ab..a777a4525f 100644
--- a/hw/timer/i8254.c
+++ b/hw/timer/i8254.c
@@ -124,6 +124,36 @@ static void pit_latch_count(PITChannelState *s)
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * 2ksbox: raise the IRQ 0 edge the guest is owed before it touches the
+ * PIT. pit_get_count() computes the count from the clock at every access,
+ * but the edge at the wrap is raised by irq_timer, which the main loop runs
+ * a wakeup late -- and a guest reading the PIT in a tight loop holds the
+ * BQL most of that time. A read in between sees the counter wrapped with
+ * no interrupt behind it, which a real 8254 shows for about a clock. A
+ * guest that keeps time from the BIOS tick count plus the counter (DOS
+ * Quake's Sys_FloatTime, Win9x's VTD) reads that as time going backward,
+ * and then counts the whole period again once the tick lands: DOS Quake
+ * ran at twice its speed in pure DOS. So an access delivers what is
+ * overdue first; an IN or OUT ends its TB, so the interrupt is taken
+ * before the guest's next instruction, as on the real part.
+ * -global isa-pit.overdue-irq=off is the A/B.
+ */
+static void pit_deliver_overdue(PITCommonState *pit)
+{
+    PITChannelState *s = &pit->channels[0];
+    int64_t now;
+
+    if (!pit->overdue_irq || !s->irq_timer || s->irq_disabled) {
+        return;
+    }
+    now = qemu_clock_get_ns(QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL);
+    /* each update moves next_transition_time past the one it was given */
+    while (s->next_transition_time != -1 && s->next_transition_time <= now) {
+        pit_irq_timer_update(s, s->next_transition_time);
+    }
+}
+
 static void pit_ioport_write(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
                              uint64_t val, unsigned size)
 {
@@ -134,6 +164,7 @@ static void pit_ioport_write(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
     trace_pit_ioport_write(addr, val);
 
     addr &= 3;
+    pit_deliver_overdue(pit);
     if (addr == 3) {
         channel = val >> 6;
         if (channel == 3) {
@@ -207,6 +238,7 @@ static uint64_t pit_ioport_read(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
         /* Mode/Command register is write only, read is ignored */
         return 0;
     }
+    pit_deliver_overdue(pit);
 
     s = &pit->channels[addr];
     if (s->status_latched) {
